      "text": "Tajique began as a 16th‑century Tiwa pueblo, part of the Tompiro group, where residents built terraced masonry homes and celebrated kachina ceremonies.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/tajique_new_mexico",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In the early 1600s, Tajique served as the seat of the San Miguel Mission, a key Franciscan center in the region.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tajique%2C_New_Mexico",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "An Apache raid in 1675 forced the abandonment of the Tajique pueblo, ending its continuous occupation before 1677.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tajique%2C_New_Mexico",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The foothills of the Manzano Mountains around Tajique expose fault‑block strata that reveal the region’s tectonic history.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
